--TEST--
Core\Jwt::signObject signs a shape and a class carrying `#[Core\Json\Derive]` as the JSON object `Core\Json::encode` writes, with `iat` and `exp` appended after it in that order — `rule:security/protocol-roster`, `rule:security/jwt-expiry-is-mandatory`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, over the two things `object` accepts. One question — "what did
// the payload segment say?" — is asked of a shape and of a deriving class, and
// the answer has to be the encoder's own document in the encoder's own field
// order with the member's two claims after it. A member that re-serialized a
// parsed copy would still print plausible claims here while writing them in
// some collation of its own, so the assertion is the payload's *prefix* rather
// than the claims read back out of it.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
final class Grant {
    public string $sub;
    public string $scope;

    public function constructor(string $sub, string $scope) {
        $this->sub = $sub;
        $this->scope = $scope;
    }
}

Core\Crypto\KeyPair $pair = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::Ed25519);

array<string> $tokens = [
    Core\Jwt::signObject(
        Core\Arr::shapeAs<{sub: string, scope: string}>(["sub" => "ada", "scope" => "read"]),
        15m,
        $pair
    ),
    Core\Jwt::signObject(new Grant("ada", "read"), 15m, $pair),
];

// A shape's class is keyed on its field names alone and that label is sorted,
// so `scope` comes before `sub`; a deriving class writes its codec's fields in
// the order they were declared. Neither is this member's choice, which is the
// point of writing the claims as the one encoder writes them.
array<string> $prefixes = [
    "{\"scope\":\"read\",\"sub\":\"ada\",\"iat\":",
    "{\"sub\":\"ada\",\"scope\":\"read\",\"iat\":",
];

foreach ($tokens as string $at => string $token) {
    array<string> $parts = Core\Str::split($token, ".");
    string $payload = Core\Encoding::decodeText(
        Core\Encoding::fromBase64Url($parts[1]),
        Core\Charset::Utf8
    );
    var $claims = Core\Json::decodeAs<{sub: string, scope: string, iat: int, exp: int}>($payload);
    if (Core\Str::startsWith($payload, $prefixes[$at])) {
        echo $claims->sub, " may ", $claims->scope, " for ", $claims->exp - $claims->iat, "s";
        echo ", in the encoder's own order", "\n";
    } else {
        echo "the payload is not the encoder's document: ", $payload, "\n";
    }
}

// The boundary on the other side: an object that wrote no member of its own.
// An optional field the shape never carried is absent from the document, so
// there is nothing for the registered pair to follow — and a member splicing
// them onto the text unconditionally writes `{,"iat":…` here.
string $bare = Core\Jwt::signObject(
    Core\Arr::shapeAs<{scope?: string}>(["other" => "ignored"]),
    15m,
    $pair
);
array<string> $empty = Core\Str::split($bare, ".");
string $written = Core\Encoding::decodeText(
    Core\Encoding::fromBase64Url($empty[1]),
    Core\Charset::Utf8
);
var $registered = Core\Json::decodeAs<{iat: int, exp: int}>($written);
if (Core\Str::startsWith($written, "{\"iat\":")) {
    echo "an empty claims object signs the two registered claims alone, ";
    echo $registered->exp - $registered->iat, "s apart", "\n";
} else {
    echo "an empty claims object wrote ", $written, "\n";
}

// And the header is the pair's, exactly as `sign`'s is: `alg` comes from the
// key and there is no argument that could have said otherwise.
echo Core\Encoding::decodeText(
    Core\Encoding::fromBase64Url(Core\Str::split($tokens[0], ".")[0]),
    Core\Charset::Utf8
), "\n";
--EXPECT--
ada may read for 900s, in the encoder's own order
ada may read for 900s, in the encoder's own order
an empty claims object signs the two registered claims alone, 900s apart
{"alg":"EdDSA","typ":"JWT"}
